// THE SUPPLIED INSTANCE ROOT, FOR A CONFINEMENT WITNESS THAT MUST NOT LIVE ON THE HOST IT DESCRIBES.
//
// A wet confinement witness proves something about a BOUNDARY by standing inside it and pushing: it installs a
// tree, writes under the confinement, and shows a write outside is refused. Both halves are WRITES, so the
// witness needs a root it is allowed to write — and until now the witnesses in this family got theirs from the
// production row: `roadmap_dashboard_instance.srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()`, whose `provision_parent_root` is
// `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent` = `/opt/gunbc-lab`. That gives a witness exactly two hosts, and neither
// one is a place a witness should be: on every host but srv1 the first `install` refuses (`install: cannot
// create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied` — nine of them on srv2, 2026-11-01), and on srv1 — which
// is LIVE, serving the lab — the claim passes by writing into the root the lab dispatches from. The row is
// filed as `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_confinement_witness_whose_only_writable_root_is_the_live_host`.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE SUPPLIES, AND WHY THIS SHAPE AND NOT ANOTHER.
//
//   THE ROOT IS AN MKTEMP LEAF UNDER A DECLARED SCRATCH PREFIX. `witness_scratch_prefix` is the only literal
//   any witness writes; the LEAF comes from `mktemp -d`, because a fixed scratch path is a rendezvous point
//   between two runs of the same witness on the same host — and a witness that inherits a tree it did not
//   create cannot tell its own write from a leftover, which is the difference between observing a confinement
//   breach and reading last week's file. The prefix is a `data` row per DESIGN section 1, and the refusal arm
//   (`witness_supplied_root_is_refused`) is a PURE decision, so it is claimed in the hermetic corpus rather
//   than only under `--wet`: the guard that keeps a witness off the live root does not itself need a host.
//
//   THE PRINCIPAL IS THE HOST'S OWN, READ AND REQUIRED — NOT ONE THE WITNESS MINTS. The alternative was
//   `groupadd`/`useradd` in the fixture. It was rejected for three reasons that all point the same way:
//   (1) /etc/passwd and /etc/group are durable host state that no scratch prefix contains, so a witness that
//   mints an account has mutated the host outside the one tree it owns, with no seal and no rollback — the same
//   defect this work removes, moved from the filesystem to the account database; (2) it needs root, and a
//   confinement witness running as root answers the DAC question with root's privilege instead of with the
//   model's declared modes, so the very assertion it exists to make stops discriminating; (3) the production
//   names (`node`, gid 1003 as measured on srv1, and `gunbc-attempt` for the shared attempt group) are facts
//   about srv1's account database — srv2 has user `briansrls`, no group `node`, and neither `gunbc-worker` nor
//   `gunbc-attempt` — so nominating them measures the host's provisioning rather than the boundary. What is
//   kept is the pattern this family already uses for the same problem (`wet_group_token` in
//   `test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness`): ask the host what account is running,
//   REQUIRE an answer, and refuse when it does not answer. `witness_principal_from_texts` is the pure decision
//   — uid, gid and both names, refused when any is missing and refused when the uid is 0 — so the refusal is
//   claimed without spawning anything, and the uid/gid/mode legs stay exactly as strong: they are graded by the
//   host (`install -o -g -m`, `find -printf '%u %g %m'`, the sandbox's own EACCES) against the MODEL's modes,
//   and now against a principal the host actually SAYS instead of one inherited from srv1.
//
//   WHAT IS *NOT* SUPPLIED, WHICH IS THE PAIRING OBLIGATION (DESIGN section 3). The instance is built from the
//   SAME production row the lab uses and the SAME production path helper it uses (`dashboard_instance_child`):
//   the deployment, the instance id, the layout, the serve function, the port, the admitted-revision source,
//   the publication branch, the worker identity, the toolchain and the memory cap all come from
//   `srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()` unchanged, so the only inputs that come from the witness are the root
//   prefix, the principal, the throwaway repository, and the transport (a witness is its own provision target;
//   `DashboardSshProvision { host: "srv1" }` would have a local test reach into the live host). The live row
//   keeps its one execution as a PURE derivation in
//   `test.claim.roadmap.dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_witness`, which asserts the shape the lab actually
//   gets — every derived path under the lab root, the owner, the group, the declared modes — without writing or
//   spawning. Deleting the real derivation makes THAT claim fail, which is what keeps supplying a root from
//   becoming, silently, deleting one.

// THE SCRATCH PREFIX EVERY WITNESS ROOT IS AN MKTEMP LEAF UNDER. A `data` row so the prefix is one admitted
// fact rather than a string scattered through fixtures; the unique leaf is always the host's, from `mktemp -d`.
data witness_scratch_prefix: NonEmptyStr = "/tmp" as NonEmptyStr

// THE LEAF TEMPLATE `mktemp -d` IS HANDED. The XXXXXX is mktemp's own token, not a placeholder: mktemp refuses
// to create the directory unless the template ends in at least three of them, and a refused mktemp is the
// fixture's `Absent` arm — a witness with no root declines, it does not fall back to a fixed path.
data witness_scratch_leaf_template: NonEmptyStr = "gunbc-wet-witness-XXXXXX" as NonEmptyStr

// THE CLAIM'S OWN LEAF, AS THE HOST NAMED IT. `mktemp -d` chose it, so nobody writing a claim can guess
// or reuse it -- which is why it is the token a probe puts inside a systemd unit name: two runs of the
// same claim cannot name the same unit, and no production attempt key can look like this. It is NOT a
// fourth copy of a `path_basename` helper (gunbc/live_deploy/member_observe.dag:163,
// gunbc/url_file_acquisition.dag:69, gunbc/gitignore_emit.dag:177 each carry one for path RENDERING,
// where an empty input or a trailing separator is a thing to tolerate); a witness leaf is a host-chosen
// name that must exist, so an unnameable leaf is returned as Absent and the claim that asked for it
// refuses.
fn witness_claim_leaf(path: String) -> String? {
  let leaf = wet_argv_out(program: basename_program_invocation, arguments: basename_argv(path: path))
  // THE REFUSALS ARE THE POINT: an empty read, or the root itself (`basename /` prints `/`), is not a
  // leaf, and a claim that cannot name its own leaf must not go on to name a systemd unit.
  if leaf == "" || leaf == "/" || string_contains(s: leaf, pattern: "/") {
    Absent
  } else {
    Present { value: leaf }
  }
}

fn witness_scratch_template() -> String {
  join([witness_scratch_prefix as String, "/", witness_scratch_leaf_template as String], "")
}

// THE HOST ROUTE, IN ONE PLACE. Three supplied-root witnesses need to run an ArgvCommand on the
// host and read its answer, and a third local copy of these three bodies was the trigger DESIGN section 6b
// names -- "the trigger is the second local implementation of one rule". They live here, beside the
// prefix and the principal commands they serve, so there is one route from a witness to `id -u`,
// `stat -c %a` and `mktemp -d`: an ArgvCommand goes in as an ArgvCommand, which is what makes a
// refusal here a real refusal and not a string comparison.
//
// STILL DUPLICATED, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT QUIET: worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness.dag:106-118
// keeps its own copies of all three and worker_git_subtree_builder_wet_witness.dag:76 its own `wet_out`
// (whose body inlines the capture call). They are not migrated here because the spawn witness's entry is
// refused today by the `circular dependency detected` in the roadmap_dashboard_instance_apply closure, so
// an edit to it could not be verified by running anything -- migrating a claim file whose claims cannot
// execute is exactly how a repair gets asserted without being observed. THE DEFECT IS NOT #15790: that PR
// landed (1a28f2a1bf01) and, by its own stated control, did NOT clear the refusal. What does not clear is
// a resolver defect in which a `decl_ref` citation STRING counts as a module-graph edge; it is routed to
// sleek-owl-397 via stern-bat-401. TRIGGER for the migration: the spawn witness's WHOLE-ENTRY resolve
// working -- not a two-claim `--functions` selection of that entry resolving, which hides the cycle
// (measured in the evidence recorded on PR #15794).
fn wet(command: ArgvCommand) -> ProcessOutcome {
  run_shell_command_capture(command: command, transport: LocalExec)
}

fn wet_ok(command: ArgvCommand) -> Bool {
  match wet(command: command) {
    ProcessRefused { exit_code: _, stderr: _ } => false
    ProcessOutputPresent { text: _ } => true
    ProcessOutputAbsent => true
  }
}

// THE WORD-LIST SIBLINGS OF wet / wet_ok / wet_out. A host operation modeled as an argv TAIL (the
// `_argv` rows in the extdeps modules: systemctl_stop_user_argv,
// basename_argv) carries no argv[0]; the caller names the program, and shell.Exec.RunArgv is the seam the
// whole fleet already uses for that shape. They exist here rather than in extdeps.exec.command because
// sealing the same words into an ArgvCommand needs a caller roster that project does not grow.
fn wet_argv_ok(program: NonEmptyStr, arguments: List<String>) -> Bool {
  // THE OUTCOME, NOT AN EXCEPTION -- and the same shape as `wet_out` below, which maps a ProcessRefused
  // to "" rather than raising. shell.Exec.RunArgv RAISES when the program cannot be spawned, and a
  // raised claim returns through the top of the interpreter: no removal of its root, no `claim-root-gone`
  // readback, no `[witness]` line (measured at 22c8f47b -- the unsandboxed control left
  // /tmp/gunbc-sandbox-unsandboxed-control-... behind on a host with no systemctl, and the only reason
  // the batch still reported it red was the leftover listing in the run script). "The host could not run
  // this argv" is a NO, and every caller of these two reads a NO as a refusal already.
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(program: program as String, arguments: arguments)
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } => false
    ShellExited => read.exit_code == 0
  }
}

fn wet_argv_out(program: NonEmptyStr, arguments: List<String>) -> String {
  // Same rule for the read side: no process, no output. A caller that expects text refuses on the empty
  // string -- `witness_claim_leaf` does -- instead of the claim dying inside a helper.
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(program: program as String, arguments: arguments)
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } => ""
    ShellExited => trim(s: read.stdout)
  }
}

fn wet_out(command: ArgvCommand) -> String {
  match wet(command: command) {
    ProcessOutputPresent { text: text } => trim(s: text)
    ProcessOutputAbsent => ""
    ProcessRefused { exit_code: _, stderr: _ } => ""
  }
}

// THE ONE PROCESS THE FIXTURE NEEDS TO GET A ROOT. Callers run this with `run_shell_command_observe` and read
// the created path off stdout; an empty or absent stdout is `Absent`, which every claim turns into a refusal
// rather than a default.
// THE CREDENTIAL A SUPPLIED UNIT IS HANDED, UNDER THE WITNESS'S OWN ROOT. The identifier is the
// route's own (OperatorCredentialFile.identifier), so the file the unit is handed and the file the
// harness looks up in $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY stay one datum; only the SOURCE moves, from the system
// credstore to a directory beside the instance the witness supplied.
fn witness_supplied_credstore_dir(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  join([instance.provision_parent_root as String, "/witness-credstore"], "")
}

fn witness_supplied_credential_source(instance: HostDashboardInstance, file: OperatorCredentialFile) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([
    witness_supplied_credstore_dir(instance: instance),
    "/",
    file.identifier as String,
    "-",
    instance.instance_id as String,
  ], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE ARM THAT MAKES THE LIVE CREDSTORE A REFUSAL, NOT A SURPRISE. Reads the FINISHED row through the
// production accessor, so a row that reintroduces WorkerDedicatedAccount -- or any future variant that
// derives a credential outside the supplied root -- is refused here, on every host, instead of reaching
// for /etc/credstore and failing inside the unit where the only diagnostic is a status code.
fn witness_supplied_credential_is_confined(scratch_parent: String, instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> Bool {
  fold(
    dispatch_worker_credentials(instance: instance),
    init: true,
    f: (ok, credential) => ok && is_under(text: credential.credstore_path as String, root: scratch_parent),
  )
}

fn witness_scratch_parent_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  mktemp_directory_command(template: witness_scratch_template() as NonEmptyStr)
}

// THE PRINCIPAL, AS THE HOST SAID IT. `uid`/`gid` are what `install -o/-g` and the readback compare; `user` and
// `group` are the names `find -printf '%u %g'` prints, which is how the modes leg checks the group it set
// (GNU find 4.10.0 exits 1 on an unknown GROUP name, so a name the host does not have is a refusal, not a
// zero).
type WitnessSuppliedPrincipal {
  uid: Int
  gid: Int
  user: NonEmptyStr
  group: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE FOUR QUESTIONS, IN FOUR PROCESSES, deliberately: one `id -u -g -n` collapses the answers into a single
// line, and then a missing group name is indistinguishable from a missing uid, which is the conflation that lets
// a witness "pass" on a host that has no such account. The first three are the sealed builders
// `extdeps.tools.id` provides for a CALLING PROCESS's own credentials (no operand: the account database cannot
// say what a running process holds); the fourth is `gunbc`'s own effective-principal read, the same
// `whoami_command` `gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_key_enrollment` asks when it must know which account a transport
// actually reached rather than which account the database names.
fn witness_uid_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  id_uid_command()
}

fn witness_gid_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  id_gid_command()
}

fn witness_group_name_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  id_group_name_command()
}

fn witness_effective_user_command() -> ArgvCommand {
  whoami_command()
}

// THE FOUR, IN ORDER, for the claim that pins that all four are asked.
fn witness_principal_commands() -> List<ArgvCommand> {
  [
    witness_uid_command(),
    witness_gid_command(),
    witness_group_name_command(),
    witness_effective_user_command(),
  ]
}

type PrincipalNumbers {
  uid: Int
  gid: Int
}

// THE PURE DECISION. Nothing here spawns, so the refusal arms are claimable in the hermetic corpus: a witness
// that silently substituted a production uid for a missing one would have to pass this first. `uid == 0` is
// refused for the reason in the header — running as root makes the DAC legs non-discriminating — and a negative
// uid or gid is what `id` prints when the account cannot be resolved, which is the host SAYING it has no such
// principal.
fn witness_principal_from_texts(
  uid_text: String,
  gid_text: String,
  user_text: String,
  group_text: String,
) -> Optional<WitnessSuppliedPrincipal> {
  match parse_principal_numbers(uid_text: uid_text, gid_text: gid_text) {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: numbers } =>
      if trim(s: user_text) == "" || trim(s: group_text) == "" || numbers.uid == 0 {
        Absent
      } else {
        Present {
          value: WitnessSuppliedPrincipal {
            uid: numbers.uid,
            gid: numbers.gid,
            user: trim(s: user_text) as NonEmptyStr,
            group: trim(s: group_text) as NonEmptyStr,
          },
        }
      }
  }
}

fn parse_principal_numbers(uid_text: String, gid_text: String) -> Optional<PrincipalNumbers> {
  match parse_int(s: trim(s: uid_text)) {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: uid } => match parse_int(s: trim(s: gid_text)) {
      Absent => Absent
      Present { value: gid } =>
        if uid < 0 || gid < 0 {
          Absent
        } else {
          Present { value: PrincipalNumbers { uid: uid, gid: gid } }
        }
    }
  }
}

// THE GUARD THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE FIXTURE. A supplied root is refused when it is, or is under, either
// production lab root or the fleet's owned install root, or when it is NOT under the declared scratch prefix.
// Over-approximation is the safe direction: a substring prefix test also refuses `/opt/gunbc-labX`, and a
// witness that has to move its scratch prefix into the model loses nothing, while a witness that slips past
// this guard writes into the live lab root. Every claim that takes a witness root checks it through this one
// decision.
fn witness_supplied_root_is_refused(candidate: String) -> Bool {
  is_under(text: candidate, root: srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent as String)
    || is_under(text: candidate, root: srv1_dashboard_lab_root as String)
    || is_under(text: candidate, root: "/opt/gunbc" as String)
    || !is_under(text: candidate, root: witness_scratch_prefix as String)
}

// A PREFIX TEST, NOT A SUBSTRING TEST, and the direction matters twice over. A substring test would accept
// `/var/tmp/gunbc-wet-witness-forged` as "under /tmp" (it contains "/tmp/"), which is a witness installing
// outside its declared prefix; and it would accept `/opt/gunbc-labX` as refused while accepting a sibling that
// merely shares a prefix with a production root. So: equal, or begins with the root followed by a separator.
fn is_under(text: String, root: String) -> Bool {
  text == root || starts_with(s: text, prefix: join([root, "/"], ""))
}

// THE INSTANCE UNDER TEST, AT THE ROOT THE WITNESS SUPPLIED. Every field that is a function of the root is
// re-derived by the SAME production helper the production derivation uses (`dashboard_instance_child`), so
// `source`, `bin/gunbc`, `origin.git`, `inbox/source.bundle`, `attempts` and `attempt-state` have the shape the
// lab's have — the leaf names and relative layout are not restated here, which is what makes this row's shape
// evidence about the production rule rather than about a fixture's idea of it. The four fields that DO come
// from the witness are named, with their reason, at the literal.
fn witness_supplied_instance(
  scratch_parent: FilePath,
  repo: FilePath,
  principal: WitnessSuppliedPrincipal,
) -> HostDashboardInstance {
  let lab = srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()
  let parent = scratch_parent as FilePath
  let root = dashboard_instance_child(root: parent, child: srv1_dashboard_lab_id)
  HostDashboardInstance {
    deployment: lab.deployment,
    instance_id: lab.instance_id,
    host_identity: lab.host_identity,
    // SUPPLIED (1 of 4): the root the witness created, not `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent`. This is the
    // field whose production value produced the srv2 reds and would have created the live lab root.
    provision_parent_root: parent,
    instance_root: root,
    local_origin_root: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "origin.git"),
    local_origin_publish_ref: lab.local_origin_publish_ref,
    // SUPPLIED (2 of 4): a throwaway repository under the same scratch tree. The production value is the lab's
    // live `source` checkout, and these claims drive `git` in this tree — so a production value here is a claim
    // running `git apply` against the lab's checkout.
    repo_root: repo,
    dispatch_base_ref: lab.dispatch_base_ref,
    publication_base_branch: lab.publication_base_branch,
    dispatch_state_scope: lab.dispatch_state_scope,
    // Re-derived, not inherited: `lab.execution_environment` carries the lab's `serve_binary` under
    // `/opt/gunbc-lab`, and a claim that execs the instance's serve binary would then exec the live host's
    // served artifact instead of the one it installed.
    execution_environment: dashboard_execution_environment(
      serve_binary: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    ),
    provider_state: lab.provider_state,
    serve_binary: dashboard_instance_child(root: root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    serve_function: lab.serve_function,
    listen_host: lab.listen_host,
    listen_port: lab.listen_port,
    tmux_server: lab.tmux_server,
    actuation: lab.actuation,
    // SUPPLIED (3 of 4): the witness IS its provision target. Inheriting the lab's value here
    // (`DashboardSshProvision { host: "srv1" }`) would have a local test reach into the live host to provision
    // it, which is the defect this row is about in its most literal form.
    actuator_host: lab.host_identity,
    // SUPPLIED (4 of 4): the throwaway repository already exists and holds the anchor, so the seed is
    // "existing repo" rather than the lab's staged bundle. The bundle path is derived from the root, so
    // keeping the lab's arm here would name a bundle under the live lab root that the witness could create.
    source_seed: DashboardExistingRepo { anchor_ref: lab.dispatch_base_ref },
    admitted_revision: lab.admitted_revision,
    toolchain: lab.toolchain,
    dispatch_worker_memory_max: lab.dispatch_worker_memory_max,
    belt_max_concurrent: lab.belt_max_concurrent,
    // THE PRINCIPAL, AS THE HOST SAID IT. `lab.directory_owner` is srv1's service account name read out of the
    // model; passing it through would have the witness assert ownership against an account database only the
    // live host has (and an absent group makes `find -chgrp` exit 1, whose zero-vs-one is not evidence about
    // the boundary). The uid and gid the claims install and read back are the host's own numbers.
    directory_owner: PosixOwnerNames {
      user: principal.user,
      group: principal.group,
    },
    // THE WORKER VARIANT IS NOT THE LAB ROW'S. srv1_lab_dashboard_instance() is
    // WorkerDedicatedAccount, and dispatch_worker_credentials turns that variant into a
    // LoadCredential= whose source is dispatch_worker_credstore_path --
    // systemd_system_credstore_directory ("/etc/credstore", a data constant in
    // extdeps.systemd.unit_file) + <identifier>-<instance id>. A supplied row that kept the variant
    // therefore asked the systemd MANAGER to read a file under /etc/credstore: on srv2, 2026-11-04,
    // that is exactly what killed launch legs of the sandbox witness with unit exit 243 (CREDENTIALS)
    // before the claim's own command ran, and /etc/credstore is a live root the witness has no
    // business touching. A supplied row carries WorkerIsServiceUser -- the variant srv1_live's own
    // fleet rows carry -- and the credential a supplied unit is handed is built under the witness's
    // own root by witness_supplied_credential_source below. Flipping this field back is not a silent
    // escape: witness_supplied_credential_is_confined reads dispatch_worker_credentials over the
    // finished row and refuses a credential whose path is not under the scratch parent.
    worker: WorkerIsServiceUser,
    materialization_store_root: lab.materialization_store_root,
  }
}

// THE ROOTS A CLAIM USING THIS INSTANCE MAY WRITE UNDER. Enumeration, not a wildcard: a claim that ends up
// writing somewhere else is a claim this set does not cover, and the inhabiting claim over the production row
// (`test.claim.roadmap.dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_witness`) is what keeps this set the same SHAPE as
// the production one. `provider_state.root` is deliberately absent — it is the account's own Codex home, the
// lab's value, and no claim in this family writes there; a claim that starts to must join this set and be
// refused by `witness_supplied_root_is_refused` if it points at a production root.
fn witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<FilePath> {
  [
    instance.provision_parent_root,
    instance.instance_root,
    instance.repo_root,
    instance.local_origin_root,
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "source"),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "bin"),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "bin/gunbc"),
    dashboard_inbox_under(root: instance.instance_root),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "attempts"),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "attempt-state"),
  ]
}

// THE TARGET A CLAIM GETS WHEN ITS ROOT IS REFUSED. A witness has no `raise`; what it has is the target it
// hands the claim. So when a witness row resolves any of its roots to a refused location, the fixture builds
// it under a prefix that cannot be written at all -- `/proc`, which is not under the scratch prefix and so is
// itself refused by the same decision -- and the claim goes RED at its first `git`/`install` instead of
// touching a tree it has no business touching. Refusing by VALUE, at the one place the target is chosen, is
// how a `Bool`-valued claim says "I will not run here".
data witness_refused_scratch_parent: NonEmptyStr = "/proc/gunbc-witness-root-refused" as NonEmptyStr

fn witness_refused_instance(repo: FilePath) -> HostDashboardInstance {
  witness_supplied_instance(
    scratch_parent: witness_refused_scratch_parent as FilePath,
    repo: repo,
    principal: witness_unnameable_principal(),
  )
}

// THE PRINCIPAL THAT CANNOT EXIST, in one place: negative ids and a `?` name, which `install -o/-g` and
// `chgrp` refuse and which no `find -printf '%u %g'` readback can match. A host that cannot say its own
// account, or a witness whose target was refused, gets this -- so a claim passes only on a principal the
// host actually named.
fn witness_unnameable_principal() -> WitnessSuppliedPrincipal {
  WitnessSuppliedPrincipal {
    uid: -1,
    gid: -1,
    user: "?" as NonEmptyStr,
    group: "?" as NonEmptyStr,
  }
}

// EVERY ONE OF THOSE ROOTS IS OFF THE LIVE HOST, and every one of them came from something the WITNESS
// supplied. The claim form of the guard, so the assertion lives with the fixture it guards and a witness cannot
// opt out of it by forgetting to check.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT THE CLAIM'S OTHER ARM IN DISGUISE. The inhabiting claim's supplied arm compares each root
// against the SCRATCH PREFIX — a corpus data row (`witness_scratch_prefix`, "/tmp"), which any path under /tmp
// satisfies. This one compares each root against the two things THIS ROW was built from: its own
// `instance_root` and its own `repo_root`. Those are different questions, and only this one catches the failure
// mode the witness is actually exposed to — a row whose one child kept a production path while the rest moved,
// or a root handed in from some third directory that happens to sit under /tmp. A prefix test asks "is this in
// the temp area"; this asks "did the witness supply it", which is the property the repair is about. And the
// second arm is not the prefix test either: it names the LIVE host, so no scratch-shaped path can satisfy it.
fn witness_supplied_roots_are_off_the_live_host(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> Bool {
  all(
    xs: witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance),
    predicate: x =>
      is_under(text: x as String, root: instance.instance_root as String)
        || x == (instance.provision_parent_root as String)
        || is_under(text: x as String, root: instance.repo_root as String),
  )
    && all(
      xs: witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance),
      predicate: x => !witness_supplied_root_is_refused(x as String),
    )
    && !any(
      xs: witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance),
      predicate: x => is_under(text: x as String, root: srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent as String),
    )
    && !any(
      xs: witness_supplied_writable_roots(instance),
      predicate: x => x == (srv1_dashboard_lab_root as String),
    )
}

// THE SAME SET, FOR THE PRODUCTION ROW — used only by the inhabiting claim, which writes nothing.
fn witness_production_writable_roots(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> List<FilePath> {
  [
    instance.provision_parent_root,
    instance.instance_root,
    instance.repo_root,
    instance.local_origin_root,
    instance.serve_binary,
    dashboard_inbox_under(root: instance.instance_root),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "attempts"),
    dashboard_instance_child(root: instance.instance_root, child: "attempt-state"),
  ]
}

// ============================= THE CLAIM-LOCAL RELEASE LOCUS =============================
// THE DRAIN DOES NOT RUN FROM A SOURCE COPY, AND srv2 PROVED WHAT THAT COST. Stern's S1 at
// `114bcf31` (2026-11-05) lost all nine root-launch legs one step before any sandbox conjunct: the
// elevated drain asked the real CLI to `run` with GUNBC_WORKSPACE_ROOT pointing at the copied guest
// tree, and the CLI answered `workspace_root: ... names no release locus: missing member
// .gunbc-tree-receipt (the tree receipt the locus install writes last; a locus without one was
// interrupted)`. The CLI is right, and it is the same rule production's own drain honours:
// `attempt_system_unit_launch_gunbc_argv_at` (dag/gunbc/roadmap/attempt_system_unit_launch.dag:575)
// passes `workspace_root: dir` where `dir` is `attempt_system_unit_launcher_dir(instance, revision)`
// = `helper_locus_dir(...)`, never a source copy; and the reader
// (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs:611) requires that directory to hold a `dag` DIRECTORY plus a regular
// file named by `release_locus_tree_receipt_name` (dag/gunbc/cli_run_workspace_root_scaffold.dag:36).
// So the supplied root gets its locus the only honest way: through the fold that installs one,
// `approval_broker_tree_install_at` (dag/gunbc/host/host_effect_realize.dag:6546). That fold installs
// the spine and ancestor dirs, the tree files at 0644 and the binaries at 0755 out of
// `cargo_default_release_dir`, then reads EVERY path back through `find | sha256sum` on both sides,
// refuses unless the two digests agree, and writes the receipt LAST through
// `approval_broker_tree_receipt_write` (:6729). Writing the receipt by hand is what DESIGN section 5
// calls a fabricated default: the file the CLI trusts would be one the test authored without ever
// installing the tree it certifies, and the "was interrupted" half of the CLI's own error message
// would stop meaning anything.
//
// THE ROUTE IS ELEVATED, AND THAT IS THE RULING, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. Every step of the fold is
// sudo-prefixed (`sudo_elevate_command` inside each `tree_install_*_step`), so installing a locus
// needs elevation. The manager's ruling on 2026-11-05 was to call the route AS-IS rather than thread
// an elevation choice into the broker's privileged install — which authorization pattern performs a
// privileged effect sits under gunbc.auth.authorization_pattern_selection, not in a caller's
// parameter. The consequence is carried in the open: on a host with no sudo the install refuses, and
// every claim that needs the locus refuses with the typed cause `witness_locus_install_refusal_cause`
// names — never green, never silently skipped. srv2 has sudo, so there the locus claims and the
// drain-shape probe go green and the skip-the-install mutant goes RED. What catches the class of
// fault in a container with no elevation is the pure discriminator
// `the_drain_names_the_installed_locus_and_not_the_source_copy` in the witness, which asserts the
// drain's own argv against these accessors and needs no elevation to do it.

// `sudo -n true`, as the host ANSWERS it. THE OUTCOME, NOT THE EXIT CODE: `shell.Exec.RunArgv` raises
// when the program cannot be spawned, and a raised claim neither refuses with a cause nor reaches its
// own cleanup — that is the shape that left srv2 holding /tmp/gunbc-sandbox-* trees at 5a4dd7e3. No
// process is a refusal, exactly as a non-zero exit is.
fn witness_sudo_elevation_available() -> Bool {
  let probe = sudo_elevate_command(command: ["/usr/bin/true"])
  let read = shell.Exec.RunArgvOutcome(
    program: probe.program.invocation as String,
    arguments: probe.arguments,
  )
  match read.outcome {
    ShellSpawnRefused { program: _, cause: _ } => false
    ShellExited => read.exit_code == 0
  }
}

// WHY THE ROOT IS HERE AND NOT WHERE PRODUCTION PUTS IT. `attempt_system_unit_launcher_locus` derives
// its base from `attempt_system_unit_launcher_helpers_root`, a LIVE-HOST path: a supplied-root claim
// that installed there would commit the exact offence this witness exists to retire, and the fixture's
// own off-the-live-host arm would refuse it. So the row keeps production's SHAPE — the same
// `helper_locus_dir` fold, and the step stem read from production's own accessor rather than
// restated — and only the base becomes the claim's instance root.
fn witness_claim_helper_locus(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> HelperLocus {
  HelperLocus {
    base: join([instance.instance_root as String, "/helper-loci"], ""),
    owner: approval_broker_helper_root_principal,
    step_stem: attempt_system_unit_launcher_locus(instance: instance).step_stem,
  }
}

// THE ONE WAY ANY CONJUNCT NAMES THE LOCUS. A write target or a run target re-derived from a literal
// here would be a second rule beside production's, and a mutation of the real derivation would then
// still find a hand-written path to pass on.
fn witness_claim_locus_dir(instance: HostDashboardInstance, revision: ReleaseRevisionBinding) -> String {
  helper_locus_dir(locus: witness_claim_helper_locus(instance: instance), revision: revision)
}

// THE BINARY THE FOLD INSTALLS IS READ FROM CARGO'S OWN RELEASE DIR UNDER THE SOURCE ROOT
// (`cargo_default_release_dir(build_root: source_root)`, joined with `gunbc_release_bin_name`), so the
// stage is where the fold looks and nowhere else. Staging is a copy of the SAME image the row's serve
// binary is planted from — the claim never gets a locus image it did not itself name.
fn witness_release_stage_dir(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  cargo_default_release_dir(build_root: instance.repo_root as String)
}

fn witness_release_stage_member(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  join([witness_release_stage_dir(instance: instance), "/", gunbc_release_bin_name], "")
}

// STAGE, DO NOT FABRICATE: `image` reaches this only from `GUNBC_WET_SERVE_BINARY` through the fleet's
// one environment read, and the caller has already required it non-empty and executable. Mode 0755 is
// the mode the fold itself installs binaries at, so the staged file and the installed one agree before
// the fold's readback has to agree them.
fn witness_stage_release_binary(instance: HostDashboardInstance, image: String) -> Bool {
  wet_ok(command: mkdir_parents_command(path: witness_release_stage_dir(instance: instance)))
    && wet_ok(command: install_file_mode_command(
      source: image,
      destination: witness_release_stage_member(instance: instance),
      mode: "0755" as NonEmptyStr,
    ))
}

// THE REVISION IS GIT'S ANSWER FOR THE TREE THE LOCUS WILL CARRY, not a string this file invents: the
// guest repo's own HEAD, read with `git rev-parse --verify HEAD` after the plant's commit landed. The
// locus is therefore named for the content it holds — and a plant whose commit never landed cannot name
// one, which is what the empty reading below refuses.
fn witness_guest_tree_revision(instance: HostDashboardInstance) -> String {
  wet_out(command: git_rev_parse_head_command(repository_dir: instance.repo_root as String))
}

// THE CAUSE A NO-ELEVATION HOST REPORTS, spelled once so the claim, the probe and the body all name
// the same reason. Chosen so a CI log cannot read it as a skip: it says what is missing and why the
// claim did not proceed.
data witness_locus_needs_elevation_cause: NonEmptyStr =
  "the locus install route needs elevation; this host has no sudo"

// PRODUCTION'S FOLD, CALLED AS-IS. The spine is the helper locus's base (the fold installs the spine
// dirs, then the ancestor of the locus, then the locus itself); `source_root` is the planted guest
// tree, whose `dag/` and `src/v2` the fold observes, installs and reads back; `executables` is exactly
// what production's helper-snapshot comment says a helper locus holds — `gunbc_release_bin_name` alone.
// The refusal arms are the two ways this claim can be unable to proceed, each with its own cause;
// neither is a pass, and the fold's own `TreeInstallRefused` names the step that failed.
fn witness_locus_install(
  instance: HostDashboardInstance,
  revision_text: String,
) -> ApprovalBrokerTreeInstallVerdict {
  let revision = RevisionBoundAtEmission { revision: revision_text as CommitSha }
  if revision_text == "" {
    TreeInstallRefused {
      step: TreeStepSourceObserve,
      reason: "the guest tree names no commit: the plant's git add/commit did not land, so there is no content to name a locus by",
    }
  } else if !witness_sudo_elevation_available() {
    TreeInstallRefused {
      step: TreeStepInstallDirectory,
      reason: witness_locus_needs_elevation_cause as String,
    }
  } else {
    approval_broker_tree_install_at(
      owner: approval_broker_helper_root_principal,
      spine: [witness_claim_helper_locus(instance: instance).base],
      source_root: instance.repo_root as String,
      locus: witness_claim_locus_dir(instance: instance, revision: revision),
      revision: revision,
      executables: [gunbc_release_bin_name],
      transport: one_transport_route(transport: LocalShell),
    )
  }
}

// DID THE FOLD CONVERGE — the same question the receipt-last ordering answers, spelled once so no
// claim has to match the production sum to ask it.
fn witness_locus_install_converged(verdict: ApprovalBrokerTreeInstallVerdict) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    TreeInstallConverged { installed_digest: _, files_installed: _ } => true
    TreeInstallRefused { step: _, reason: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE CAUSE, OUT OF THE VERDICT — so a claim's log line says which arm refused instead of leaving a
// bare `false` behind a 245-second leg.
fn witness_locus_install_refusal_cause(verdict: ApprovalBrokerTreeInstallVerdict) -> String {
  match verdict {
    TreeInstallConverged { installed_digest: _, files_installed: _ } => ""
    TreeInstallRefused { step: step, reason: reason } => join([tree_install_step_name(step: step), ": ", reason], "")
  }
}

// THE THREE PATHS THE CLI ASKS ABOUT, ALL OFF THE ONE ACCESSOR. The fold's readback already proved the
// digests agree; these are the paths the drain, the probe and the presence conjuncts all read, so no
// conjunct re-derives a locus path from a literal and a mutation of the real derivation has nowhere
// left to hide. `dir_text` is the same text `witness_claim_locus_dir` yields for the binding, reached
// from the revision text the claim read out of git.
fn witness_locus_dir_text(instance: HostDashboardInstance, revision_text: String) -> String {
  helper_locus_dir(
    locus: witness_claim_helper_locus(instance: instance),
    revision: RevisionBoundAtEmission { revision: revision_text as CommitSha },
  )
}

// The `dag` DIRECTORY the reader requires (cli_run.rs:611), inside the locus.
fn witness_locus_dag_dir(instance: HostDashboardInstance, revision_text: String) -> String {
  join([witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, revision_text: revision_text), "/", "dag"], "")
}

// The receipt FILE the reader requires, named by production's constant and no other spelling.
fn witness_locus_receipt_path(instance: HostDashboardInstance, revision_text: String) -> String {
  join(
    [
      witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, revision_text: revision_text),
      "/",
      release_locus_tree_receipt_name,
    ],
    "",
  )
}

// The binary the drain execs and the probe execs, named by `gunbc_release_bin_name` — the same name the
// fold installed it under, so the drain cannot quietly begin running the row's serve binary instead.
fn witness_locus_binary_path(instance: HostDashboardInstance, revision_text: String) -> String {
  join(
    [
      witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, revision_text: revision_text),
      "/",
      gunbc_release_bin_name,
    ],
    "",
  )
}

// BOTH SOURCE ROOTS THE DRAIN PASSES, as production's argv builder spells them: each entry of
// `release_locus_tree_subdirs` joined under the locus dir. A drain that named the source copy here
// would fail the reader for the same reason srv2's did.
fn witness_locus_source_roots(instance: HostDashboardInstance, revision_text: String) -> List<String> {
  map(
    release_locus_tree_subdirs,
    sub => join([witness_locus_dir_text(instance: instance, revision_text: revision_text), "/", sub], ""),
  )
}
